Collection:
-used to store data
-data structure (like container)
-stores elements in memory
-an add, remove, update
-can be traversed multiple times
- uses imperative programming (loops)
-direct operations (add, remove)
-(immediate execution)
-Parallel Processing in
 Collection → not directly






Stream:
-used to process data
 -sequence of operations (pipeline)
 -does not store data, works on existing data
 - cannot modify original data
 -an be used only once
- uses functional programming (lambda)
-operations like:
 filter()
 map()
 sorted()
 reduce()
 -Lazy (runs only when terminal operation is called)
 -Parallel Processing in
Collection → not directly
Stream → supports parallel processing


Collection is used to store data in memory and is mutable,
 while Stream is used to process that data in a functional
 and lazy manner without storing it.
 Streams cannot modify the original data and can be consumed only once."

